From: buntOS contributors
Subject: [PATCH] regulator: mt6363: Cancel the overcurrent work on removal

The overcurrent interrupt handler disables the interrupt and schedules a
delayed work to enable it again, but nothing stops that work when the
driver goes away, so it can run after the module is unloaded. Mark Brown
asked about this on the MT6373 driver, which has the same code.

Set the work up with devm_delayed_work_autocancel() before registering the
regulator. Managed resources go in the reverse order, so the regulator goes
first and frees the interrupt, which then cannot schedule the work again
after it was cancelled. The mapping of the interrupt goes last, as before.

Link: https://lore.kernel.org/r/agKKuH0fP3lBMuFd@sirena.co.uk
Fixes: 3c36965df808 ("regulator: Add support for MediaTek MT6363 SPMI PMIC Regulators")
---
 drivers/regulator/mt6363-regulator.c | 7 ++++++-
 1 file changed, 6 insertions(+), 1 deletion(-)

diff --git a/drivers/regulator/mt6363-regulator.c b/drivers/regulator/mt6363-regulator.c
--- a/drivers/regulator/mt6363-regulator.c
+++ b/drivers/regulator/mt6363-regulator.c
@@ -850,7 +850,12 @@
 			return ret;
 
 		config.driver_data = info;
-		INIT_DELAYED_WORK(&info->oc_work, mt6363_oc_irq_enable_work);
+
+		/* Must outlive the regulator, which owns the IRQ that schedules it */
+		ret = devm_delayed_work_autocancel(dev, &info->oc_work,
+						   mt6363_oc_irq_enable_work);
+		if (ret)
+			return ret;
 
 		rdev = devm_regulator_register(dev, &info->desc, &config);
 		if (IS_ERR(rdev))
